#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad5e1f00-cc1f-5d4f-87c2-02b6b36f47ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,731" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399175} {:answer/id #uuid "cec7db82-de58-50eb-a3aa-1156e21fbeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\,332" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399176} {:answer/id #uuid "34503797-dc86-5eff-9dba-4c2c0e4b5865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22\\,377" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399177} {:answer/id #uuid "9c43e127-b476-5072-8782-64defe890eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\,248" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399178} {:answer/id #uuid "96af2a00-4233-59fc-a447-2f18d08406ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31\\,755" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399176} :answer-field/id #uuid "714a5ee8-c0ff-5aa3-a5d9-82c81856f170" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c60fbb60-b78a-5c24-90ef-165c91d4dd65" :question/math-academy-id "q-83144" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a video channel after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nIf the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, calculate the number of subscribers after $12$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, so we have ${S}_{0}=15\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of subscribers when $t=12,$ we substitute $t=12$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(12) & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05\\cdot 12} \\\\  & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.6} \\\\  & ≈27\\,331.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of subscribers is $27\\,332$ after $12$ weeks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "badd1b57-14e7-5b56-b3e2-4f76bf483962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399338} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc1b791-33fe-5cbe-8db2-7cec317b3415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399339} {:answer/id #uuid "9947a22d-6748-5635-9bff-c054c9d5412e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399340} {:answer/id #uuid "a32a5598-4875-50b4-a207-219749ce447a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399341} {:answer/id #uuid "39ea83b4-6e8e-5133-9645-9dd52053a38b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399338} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d542a7-e1e6-5040-8811-6896030adc94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99338d6e-762d-5e46-924e-c9b8ff055bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-99009" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f5f9e59-3c36-5807-b8f1-60d5c867aee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399582} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ef51c8-be4a-5a5f-b4b6-7b12be0bb5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399583} {:answer/id #uuid "3f26a705-024d-5901-899b-988f84681bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399584} {:answer/id #uuid "49678131-628e-553a-9844-5179f76e8b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399585} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d4bea4-992f-5fb1-be9b-5bc63898ddea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399584} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c7c9128-4613-5828-914f-598e6ef01189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d44d9a6b-035e-5168-a3f6-72ab2e21a7d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106878" :question/problem "The rate at which a body cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the body in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-21).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ffa0ff-df0b-5bc5-b896-3f6772a35019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$28$ and $24$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399852} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1177f-3e11-593f-ad9c-dda3299fba50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$24.5$ and $18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399853} {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} {:answer/id #uuid "ee829b75-97f1-545e-8cf4-3704340f4ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$49$ and $36$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399855} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e390e1-9f85-5321-a954-3f147b97e82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} :answer-field/id #uuid "44102d96-20a4-51fc-add2-7a7a026df6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3113828e-6ca6-530b-804d-2cdc91aade73" :question/math-academy-id "q-115134" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73b6602d-f037-52b0-8c48-d0885dedafc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=2(x+9{e}^{-3x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399991} {:answer/id #uuid "30b063ed-08ab-5694-9771-9427e9a07c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=x-2{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399992} {:answer/id #uuid "95477359-7c43-51b4-a1ff-890600150acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=x+{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399993} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2d9c2f-bf87-54d1-a5c5-0103894f75e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=x-18{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399994} {:answer/id #uuid "3694a7a7-cd54-5712-bdcb-421abc079139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=2(x-3{e}^{-3x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399992} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f36605a-43ff-547f-a708-ab078c73b400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e422fe9-fc97-5df0-b6a5-ce1fd6f9f5cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-119037" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)-6{e}^{-3x}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+6{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+6{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+6\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+6(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =x-2{e}^{-3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755a881c-6b14-5e96-bd68-6af58d5ef813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401539} {:answer/id #uuid "5965e390-56a0-55ef-a7dc-eff34c09cc53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401540} {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1fc5c2-bdfa-52e9-a59e-c6227d171448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401542} {:answer/id #uuid "47a16fa9-9d49-55d0-a143-c2b6cca5c4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41dd28d-0ca0-5222-9cfe-700ddaa37f17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e791f5b6-5e18-534a-99e5-45cc16c1809f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16455" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=1$ and ${b}^{2}=8.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=1,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=8,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=1.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402135} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe81525-8780-55b2-9e79-394a40fd9a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$50$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402136} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9dd8e3-5764-58b2-9ff2-4b88c32898f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$40$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402137} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5c6a61-12a4-5352-8fcc-e3319bbc18bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402138} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb667f2-6fca-5729-a7fd-9dfe18862602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$28$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402135} :answer-field/id #uuid "63531a29-5d3e-5f5f-97ce-ef01b7d03202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4fd0915-73cc-575a-b3cc-4a156a96dd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-335542" :question/problem "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{25}{36}\\right) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{25}{36}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{18}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{18}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{18}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{25}{36}\\right)}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a62be6e9-5300-5837-bb21-616f9afabd11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$570$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402142} {:answer/id #uuid "52f73195-6ad4-5063-9185-1d0ce506d0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$560$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402143} {:answer/id #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402144} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb7ad78-9e83-5378-a008-fb209e8c0c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$550$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402145} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3362f7-e0ab-59c9-813b-230e3fd73524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$590$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402144} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e99660a-207e-56cc-af0c-7810e8c04e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ee0a87d3-d3e9-539a-9074-68a374d85d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335590" :question/problem "A small bakery produces loaves of bread. The owner knows that the bakery can produce a maximum of $600$ loaves a day; however, current production is below this maximum. Since she started monitoring, the bakers produced $480$ loaves and $500$ loaves on days $4$ and $8,$ respectively. How many loaves do they produce on day $40?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the bread production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the bread production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible bread production and its current bread production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the bakery, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ loaves, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(600-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{600-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{600-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $4,$ the bread production was $480$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(4)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & =480 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $8,$ the bread production was $500$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(8)=500,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(8) & =500 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-8k} & =500 \\\\ K{e}^{-8k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-8k}} & =\\frac{120}{100} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} & =120 \\\\ K & =120{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} \\\\ K & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=600-144{e}^{-(t/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of loaves baked on day $t=40$ is\n\n$$\nE(40)=600-144{e}^{-(40/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}≈576.743…≈577\\,\\text{loaves},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f7ba4e-e115-562d-8f0b-e2fb3c50999d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402149} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} {:answer/id #uuid "3c36fd19-5696-5a1e-9dc9-b04f43bf5c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402151} {:answer/id #uuid "31324eeb-37b1-5843-9dfa-024be7517c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402152} {:answer/id #uuid "11e8a292-e74d-5f79-93dd-d159881ed802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} :answer-field/id #uuid "450a3a23-c54b-5ed0-90a2-deef088dea63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "44cded13-6bc7-52ea-8fcc-d6fe583c3d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-335619" :question/problem "A national park is monitoring the number of people who have downloaded a new park app. The system can support a maximum of $800$ users, but the total is still below this limit. Since the park began tracking downloads, the number of users reached $620$ on day $3$ and $720$ on day $9.$ On what day will the number of users reach $760?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of users, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of app users $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of users the system can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ users, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the number of users was $620.$ So, applying the condition $P(3)=620,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =620 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-3k} & =620 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the number of users was $720.$ So, applying the condition $P(9)=720,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =720 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-9k} & =720 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{180}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} & =180 \\\\ K & =180{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} \\\\ K & =180\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ K & =180\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-270{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$  be the day when the app reaches $760$ users. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =760 \\\\ 800-270{e}^{-kd} & =760 \\\\ 40 & =270{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{40}{270} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{4}{27} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{27}\\right) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{27}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the number of users will be $760$ on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{27}\\right)}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)}=14.1285…≈14,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7b08f4-fd50-5010-9da5-db54d62d9cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3$ or $-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403160} {:answer/id #uuid "4baca1b8-62e7-5f52-af30-1fc3884ec93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ or $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403161} {:answer/id #uuid "989a9e5a-82d4-559e-b64e-15fdf78c6beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403162} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbdf5da-2897-576f-842c-af25e23e73b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403163} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a7a515-e8e3-5996-959a-f3908110ac5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-6$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403161} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa513b8-ca41-5963-9d1c-ec92073b4073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a341ef36-052b-58a5-8e5a-622fd4d5ea6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64701" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-15y=0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-15{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa22294c-97df-59d3-a2ef-bb723379bd93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404233} {:answer/id #uuid "05c76659-9ee2-5ea1-9921-4c61064bc47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2)/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404234} {:answer/id #uuid "d957c339-7d86-5b49-bfb4-f34772448576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1)/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404235} {:answer/id #uuid "59acccda-8ad0-5671-8d3b-1eba77285637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404236} {:answer/id #uuid "08886f8e-1110-502c-b6b0-62359e9dd0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4)/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404237} :answer-field/id #uuid "5399864b-fdb6-560a-bb1c-eadfa2e8485b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "177e97c5-b0fe-5fdf-abe1-80b42d3faf53" :question/math-academy-id "q-8306" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2yx+y{x}^{2}$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2yx+y{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(2x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=1$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =1 \\\\ K{e}^{{1}^{2}+{1}^{3}/3} & =1 \\\\ K{e}^{4/3} & =1 \\\\ K & ={e}^{-4/3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2yx+y{x}^{2}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4/3}\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3}={e}^{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4)/3}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5069fb02-022e-544e-b7ca-c45166ca4bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404268} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcfa690-e3cd-5cc8-9177-2dc5f0ed3023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404269} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec33d3c-cfb8-5e66-a78e-a3afbcab3a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404270} {:answer/id #uuid "2b98ed15-9816-5e8c-8df1-651d5c3a7b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404271} {:answer/id #uuid "e5065de5-7957-55f6-bf19-b00b1fba3806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{y-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404270} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19317f0-552c-5bb1-aa73-1ed888b17d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "29260781-8760-5b93-aff1-74357dc9fb2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8369" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}-(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}+(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c59fbbc-2dc0-562b-b432-ce3c7f59dad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ or $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404498} {:answer/id #uuid "c01408ce-3680-5eb5-8582-5d15eb08699d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404499} {:answer/id #uuid "0e45d023-bccd-58f6-9704-f559be5f8da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-6$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404500} {:answer/id #uuid "1b463397-33ab-5e64-907b-e8904f8a0935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404501} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e036bd-fcc3-5ff2-9e88-b80ab4086884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404502} :answer-field/id #uuid "117667a9-4c0a-559e-becf-2c255a4fa945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8bfd769-670f-5f6b-bec7-13134d36de0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90706" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea90192a-98aa-5857-a246-925646efadb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404505} {:answer/id #uuid "58fb9126-dcca-5a22-be00-7d49e5cf7948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404506} {:answer/id #uuid "dad7dae3-861a-5560-878f-de18703b5976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{-5x}-{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404507} {:answer/id #uuid "28ac766a-b6d1-5aa1-a97b-4a4386277974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404508} {:answer/id #uuid "9709ebe4-8c4a-5c48-8164-358a57e70e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{-5x}+3{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404506} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1363158-fb5d-52a2-aee6-d038f5cfedc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec0270d1-70c2-5347-87eb-345d880dbd64" :question/math-academy-id "q-90725" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+15y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-5.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{5x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2348e74d-3666-5c00-ab7c-13b1cc040188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421253} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd0d2ab-5b95-54c5-81e4-e970978944e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421265} {:answer/id #uuid "69383b24-f13a-51a1-ab0a-dc656419036c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421268} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc287fc-d96a-5e8a-b37e-db12d52fb251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421271} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e694a4-9850-5329-8699-d8c145b68e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421268} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ff275d-27d3-5143-9da4-76df4f6e09e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "16b925fc-3e3d-5f10-942e-39dcf0cbf5aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-129803" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({e}^{x}){(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546a4ff5-ae08-5b2d-af1e-53c8fe140e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421263} {:answer/id #uuid "85790f7f-c60c-58be-9db4-3349cd6ff648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421274} {:answer/id #uuid "85a26b10-da99-59e3-809f-f023dad31c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421275} {:answer/id #uuid "c850c71d-7d08-5a39-bf9b-f0c43f99ce6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421275} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e342e39-3482-5d77-a23d-de51cf8fa0e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421297} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a517c0a-31b2-510c-8c55-64c0349a20c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421250} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2481cb-a2dd-599c-b082-f5aa8177b9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421251} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f38bb7-a295-503f-99c8-be2e7ecf6bfb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421299} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "600b1bd0-4a54-55ee-8f3d-ea639c33d64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421266} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5e65e4-0589-5686-9096-d9440bea556a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421270} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a2b6ff-740b-5c73-94e2-69f51c63499e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3b78d46d-4b29-5944-9afd-75dc247769fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334119" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=-3x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3x & x+1 \\\\ (-3x)^{′} & (x+1)^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3x & x+1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3x)(1)-(x+1)(-3) \\\\  & =-3x+3(x+1) \\\\  & =-3x+3x+3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efc36190-e9b2-59a8-90c3-e42746b56e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474756} {:answer/id #uuid "64273c85-b54b-59a4-85ac-81ca202e2b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474757} :answer-field/id #uuid "f069e89d-9efc-5468-a700-6f1c190427d9" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475304} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c223e50-374d-53fb-9ef7-5a76d425cc83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474758} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd04d69-7309-5a80-8141-f52da0de1b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474758} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c72983f-bc9a-5d8a-a9d9-c1192271f6a9" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475305} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "849b82db-c774-5be6-b08a-ea48ead49bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474760} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a3326f-c419-5d83-add3-70561f8b99a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474760} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef9fe9d-abab-5748-a003-4777d555bb38" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475306} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "879c0bb5-661f-591a-9f87-49fc9ee4c018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669159} {:answer/id #uuid "c40bd71f-949b-531a-905d-73ae0c3bd3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669159} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e90d464-c485-59a8-b236-772aefacd6da" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "46d6b627-57fc-5c4f-9c8a-dc31a7241e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334252" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2)-x(2x)+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1918dcc5-9c9c-5d4c-8fb6-13ae3046b611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421249} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c2bd37-d688-5185-bc0d-d9a7b5aa24e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421286} {:answer/id #uuid "dff2af51-6b63-5e15-a1dd-5250c8ddde2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421249} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3ff0cc-2f32-5f3d-8c4a-1cfe74f94acc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421298} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c39ed1-3288-5a6c-931d-fabc4fa8ff45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421259} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9480d9-9bac-5230-b263-f51496c63b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421289} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bfcf71a-fd64-5c92-96e0-a4da19acec22" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421300} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44448b71-836e-5481-a958-fbda8470d5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421260} {:answer/id #uuid "e71ff228-1aa6-58e3-ac69-ec58903aae39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421292} :answer-field/id #uuid "77d6349d-b35a-5cd4-9174-8afd3686d64b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421301} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c7fbe1-9c5b-5cb0-b4a6-9f060759a858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421245} {:answer/id #uuid "31b49dd0-7130-505e-8975-5b6b86b0fd97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421256} {:answer/id #uuid "6f826a5b-626f-57ae-8de0-45b178d28a30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421256} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb37fa92-34ce-5da3-9cc2-7439e9823eb3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421305} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2197f3d2-6e07-5ebe-bd6c-f3c30c3e35f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421252} {:answer/id #uuid "8e130257-ffbb-5f17-ab93-5d676d75e801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421276} {:answer/id #uuid "a63475ff-0ba5-51bf-a4b7-d1c5078139e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421279}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421276} :answer-field/id #uuid "e75553da-6c04-50a4-a94c-aab86bb8cabd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47c9e11d-3763-56a1-a6fd-21b1255f236b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334011" :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{e}^{x},\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nFor all $x\\in R,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ba94b5-d5f6-561b-b2e2-a78ef02bf1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{k}_{1}+{k}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421246} {:answer/id #uuid "b048ca70-d1f0-56c3-a28c-d0e5a258697c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{k}_{1}-{k}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421280} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b7e62e-b69d-5648-8b53-98d08560d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{k}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421281} {:answer/id #uuid "e36b9598-f179-5762-9811-35f0a0c1da39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{k}_{1}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421291} :answer-field/id #uuid "01275dc9-5156-5923-9402-205cfa0d2994" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2de4acfa-51aa-5671-b0d3-3dfe17c3742f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421255} {:answer/id #uuid "57e2ee3c-9499-560b-ba1a-25d245e37439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421255} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3a14a1-7676-561a-9298-96af99e9a3d3" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421296} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178c87ca-598d-5627-943a-1799fc01e9f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{k}_{1}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421247} {:answer/id #uuid "639e84e6-88a8-529a-ab9b-7db0dd64d408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421267} {:answer/id #uuid "d246637c-9fd4-5cb4-ad70-d9b3c08984f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{k}_{1}+2{k}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421287} {:answer/id #uuid "d43d40ae-c0f5-5deb-b6d2-989b5e2bdaae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{k}_{1}-2{k}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421267} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bdf06db-d344-5a02-bd9e-aa29815929d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421302} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e8e28e-3e7f-54f7-b310-9eb1ff3e1e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{k}_{1}={k}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421254} {:answer/id #uuid "3dfd0190-a805-5bc2-8860-895586bac109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${k}_{1}=0$ and ${k}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421257} {:answer/id #uuid "b87fd0e7-ea30-5c71-ba81-ab9706b55310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${k}_{1}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ and ${k}_{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421282} {:answer/id #uuid "eda2b773-f3f3-5a89-95e0-bd3a01c431a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{k}_{1}={k}_{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421254} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1bf545f-c2ab-544f-8c0d-297f8adf4142" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421303} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1886b4c5-85d1-54e5-8510-86fe8c320098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421248} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b9589-1097-5d3e-a736-dde5e2438be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421248} :answer-field/id #uuid "c28908a1-f4e3-564f-a061-6af102ecf123" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c75ba2f7-e873-5513-857a-f8ee74ebb547" :question/math-academy-id "q-334026" :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${{e}^{x},\\,x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nSuppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{k}_{2}\\cdot x=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${k}_{1}$ and ${k}_{2}$ are constants.\n\nFor example:\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=0,$ we get  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get  \n{{field-2}}.\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is {{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}{e}^{x}+{k}_{2}x=0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(0)=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\n- If $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=0\\,⇒\\,2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=0.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{independent}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c37fde0-293d-5fff-8b41-8792f1c3b2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421550} {:answer/id #uuid "60162dda-59fc-569f-9bbc-f89a1da9b64e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421551} {:answer/id #uuid "650d25a5-3c4b-5948-9c9f-4ca92962040c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421552} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4b634a-7190-518b-8a3e-0e64b953f99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421553} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ee88ea-070f-5d68-aaa9-8fb89bd88756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{7}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421550} :answer-field/id #uuid "a872ab99-4b04-549f-a3e0-8010a8cc8418" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "05225557-6476-5115-9d07-1e8467a995db" :question/math-academy-id "q-73631" :question/problem "![](images/39/3989e71a908c5536d97732e253effe16fadb6382a90610db0a8693eec8161393.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b840a02-6e20-5e04-b9ba-9bc1e18883f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-5$ or $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421908} {:answer/id #uuid "41908330-21d9-59d9-a0b2-a53e1593c36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ or $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421909} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb31420-e922-5f6e-bedb-6f1ece717ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421913} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa54804-bed0-557a-ba9a-e0d7bcc11265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ or $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421916} {:answer/id #uuid "912dd08d-5db0-5a08-ab81-5772fdca1e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421916} :answer-field/id #uuid "dafe6fcf-90f7-529a-8290-8d2af8b5a7f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57b50761-0ebc-5773-b974-58926652c62b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129835" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}+35y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+35{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +35) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +35 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =5$ or $\\lambda =7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47caa681-8680-5312-9d36-19b2868656e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421911} {:answer/id #uuid "4deac328-5c08-5937-b799-6f57cf09acfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421912} {:answer/id #uuid "646c2a9c-ee6b-59ef-abca-48eaa42e7b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-x}-7{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421914} {:answer/id #uuid "a500bf2b-0bcf-52aa-8621-3db9d47c8750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}+{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421919} {:answer/id #uuid "f5419660-dfd9-56ab-8bf3-1a8501f74f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}+{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421919} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c296597-6c06-5bf8-aa85-d5015907d1ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab1d8df9-7551-58b4-b23c-3a36ae997ae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130122" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}-7y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-7{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -7) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =7.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-x}$ and ${e}^{7x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{6x}+{e}^{7x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43359a46-a31b-5979-b00d-e832c7aab359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421910} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5b35fd-08b6-534e-aef6-d64720abed6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421915} {:answer/id #uuid "8b32650e-a838-5e42-b674-904b88326a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{12x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421917} {:answer/id #uuid "b72954b9-0f27-5ed9-92fd-03be2ce3c9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421920} {:answer/id #uuid "c1df491c-fb96-5d15-b8cb-b70016700d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421921} :answer-field/id #uuid "390cf455-f96c-59d6-8b71-3d6a053b53ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aecf7af1-6026-52e6-a339-611dfc1550ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-90715" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+12{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +12) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +12=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =3$ or $\\lambda =4.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bda799f-2dc1-508a-b332-a2dc15ad6469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{-5t}-4{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427566} {:answer/id #uuid "463b0c74-3f5f-5ede-984f-20e81cf9dab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-5t}+6{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427567} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3822e6-4ea3-5bd8-88d6-1f35c1d51f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-5t}+2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427568} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd12f7d-a1de-5498-8a07-57a3b71dfca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427569} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c2bf7c-2f4c-5e0e-8a59-5fd6f0e6f6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427568} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1bdcac-c8f7-5ea7-bb80-b176a1307c71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "128c4108-f47c-515a-aaac-ed93eb92e39c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64708" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-10y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-10{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-5$ or $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-5t}$ and ${e}^{2t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $-3{e}^{-5t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf94a0c-0e29-57f1-b831-528166c238c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432569} {:answer/id #uuid "dd836c36-70c9-53c0-b7fa-96d4837068ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432569} :answer-field/id #uuid "3672eb02-0003-53d9-90a8-1a9105690abc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432573} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e9135b-b871-5aba-b40d-ba3025b92b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432557} {:answer/id #uuid "273ff444-7dd9-5e5a-b414-0596922f638a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432560} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5b64e6-9a77-5e45-9c0d-3ad872a7c207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432561} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1519be-802b-5b17-9751-f17a087eadf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432562}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432557} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbf6a1b-898e-56e9-9f12-6a5e1d80b975" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432575} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab65ccb-a918-513f-ad62-ae4b2896ed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432558} {:answer/id #uuid "1e50b1fc-9bb0-519a-87b4-b41bfcf588bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432558} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6385f95-6b30-5136-8980-da81fcd668ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10c6f8f6-9fca-5e23-8276-22a855906488" :question/math-academy-id "q-334111" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} & {(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9434ee35-97a1-5285-97bd-642508f58883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474768} {:answer/id #uuid "2e62f471-e501-527c-a2bf-b58c36ed0de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474769} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52c9065-8b6f-5163-be58-9a1c7fadbe10" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475310} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee5d6baf-53f3-5f60-82f6-8b26db329f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474770} {:answer/id #uuid "87258704-04de-53fe-86be-3991a51c9d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474771}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474771} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f5919f8-4a8b-57fc-ba5e-3d0f046a50c8" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475311} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a4f9e18-0783-5aa4-ba7f-a403d553756b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474772} {:answer/id #uuid "b9af53cc-7fb3-530b-a444-9b33c32f55d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474773}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474773} :answer-field/id #uuid "da939024-b939-5a26-a4f7-b9817e50d965" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475312} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31f59168-4cfc-56af-bf7f-019fc0d12390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669163} {:answer/id #uuid "583b5b43-6df7-5d81-8579-cea13e85d426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669163} :answer-field/id #uuid "88133f31-18b8-58be-acbb-c90966099ef5" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "248767b0-4e8f-59a2-a18a-6d7a3f7d1129" :question/math-academy-id "q-334262" :question/problem "$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+6x{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x).$ Notice that $\\frac{1}{x}={x}^{-1},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)+6x\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{2}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x).$ Notice that $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{\\frac{1}{2}},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2})+6x(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2})-2\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ (-{x}^{1/2}+3{x}^{1/2}-2{x}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$” is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{-1} & {x}^{1/2} \\\\ -{x}^{-2} & \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-{x}^{1/2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}+{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$”; “${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$”; “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bacd32ae-566a-595f-ae61-c7390064179f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474762} {:answer/id #uuid "af9b7df9-824a-5976-8ef0-0ed97e33de24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474762} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b52e612-ea71-5a2e-a81c-18901ac0011f" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475307} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9146691-1ccc-57f9-88b9-54805a3f76b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474764} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb64cb4-e2ff-50e2-a176-9905722d9d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474765} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f934a8-f5e6-5690-a1d4-2746d5ccf5fa" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c25a7c82-ba97-5324-83d9-8843be6f251d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474766} {:answer/id #uuid "97e7e003-0f8d-59ae-a1f3-79ac064de7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474766} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb9f336-1bf0-5e57-b4b7-e8e56b5843aa" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3afcad9-b96a-542d-93d2-13dc85849129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669161} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a48733-9d35-5dca-abec-a33a03d053a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669161} :answer-field/id #uuid "782071e7-63ab-57f8-8b98-9927a0c4f6ea" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8005871f-5013-58a1-b9ee-7ef831d491ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-334257" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-12y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}}\\right)+2x\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\right)-12\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x)+2x(3{x}^{2})-12({x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dcd62ed-d1d5-5d66-b9a7-d3752b72ffcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434263} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f59ee40-6a49-5f76-9b09-b56f1e7b8c65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb3f03d-cfef-5f9f-b396-26b221d4f856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434266} :answer-field/id #uuid "62dc0b83-a470-59d2-b62b-b5ccbaa907c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c46acbe-8af8-5805-a582-9ade9300c9a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-279199" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{13}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=13$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=13,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{13}=2\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "224b79cf-bf9d-546e-92e5-652da96853b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434264} {:answer/id #uuid "239820f5-492c-5074-bb16-a6af5ed54f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434265} {:answer/id #uuid "53b5a01d-de71-5112-abcb-3f57176855d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434267} {:answer/id #uuid "64c8fe0e-8e01-5ebd-869d-3e05d36b78cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434269} {:answer/id #uuid "9428f7fa-4636-5ad9-ac5c-92e2a7fbe6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434270} :answer-field/id #uuid "29540716-87b2-5957-a8c4-7141164fdefb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5fbef9b7-68b9-5b6d-beff-b38a63574b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16458" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2adb84964a605495c84921943ec76f9820ba18851cda817037dd6022808d1fd7.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb06d992-e82f-5977-a714-2f2fd87616b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434271} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f8d6b7-9861-51ad-96d2-5cd3b96789b2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434272} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56513915-f193-5d63-936d-07a8b133ceae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434268} :answer-field/id #uuid "878be2a7-5abd-5dc4-9fe0-30ce370e3435" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb8398f9-e97a-58eb-bd8b-b49ccd2d689d" :question/math-academy-id "q-279167" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10.$"}]}
